# Optional: /etc/fstab inside the *pi* distro.
#
# Exposes ONE directory from your daily dev distro — its ~/DEV — so pi can work
# on the same files without giving it the dev distro (or Windows) wholesale.
# Everything else stays invisible.
#
# Do NOT hand-write this. Generate the exact line with:
#
#   DEV_DISTRO=<dev-distro-name> DEV_USER=<dev-user> ./mount-dev.sh --print-fstab
#
# Test the mount manually first (see mount-dev.sh), then paste the generated
# line below and run: sudo mount -a
#
# Requirements:
#   - the daily dev distro must be running, or the mount fails
#   - replace <DEV_DISTRO> with the name from `wsl -l -v`
#   - replace <DEV_USER>   with your Linux user in that distro
#   - 1000:1000 assumes the pi user is uid/gid 1000 (adjust if not)
#
# Verify the path first (do not add to fstab until this succeeds):
#
#   sudo mkdir -p /mnt/dev
#   sudo mount -t drvfs '\\wsl.localhost\<DEV_DISTRO>\home\<DEV_USER>\DEV' /mnt/dev
#   ls /mnt/dev
#
# Generated line goes here:
# \\wsl.localhost\<DEV_DISTRO>\home\<DEV_USER>\DEV  /mnt/dev  drvfs  defaults,uid=1000,gid=1000,umask=022  0 0
